From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 17:51:23 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] media: virtio-video: add the Kernarium host camera

The emulator offers the Apple host's cameras as a local experimental
Virtio device, ID 65533, which speaks the Virtio video protocol of the
codecs with camera commands added. The existing driver binds it and
registers, for each camera, a V4L2 capture node and a metadata node,
from /dev/video10 up, so the decoder and the encoder stay below it in
the window Android's codec2 scans.

Opening a node starts nothing: a file creates its host stream when it
sets a format or asks for buffers, and every open file has a stream of
its own. NV12 is a video stream at a frame rate the device offers, MMAP
or DMABUF. JPEG and the Bayer formats are stills, one capture per
queued buffer; a cookie in the timestamp's seconds and the LAST buffer
flag group the buffers that are exposed together. The metadata node
returns one exposure record per buffer, matched to frames by sequence.
A frame's timestamp is its age subtracted from the driver's clock; a
still is stamped when it completes, because the time of its exposure is
in the metadata.

The device's controls, with their ranges, are registered for each file
of a camera and reach the camera through any of its streams. A control
needs a stream, so a camera keeps a metadata stream for it, which lets a
torch be written with no capture running. V4L2_CID_PRIVACY and a
read-only compound control with the camera's description record are
answered by the driver.

A system suspend stops the streams that hold buffers, which keeps the
device from being reset, and the resume queues the buffers again. If the
device was reset anyway, the resume creates the streams, writes the
controls and attaches the buffers again.

The nodes and what they use outlive the device's removal for as long as
a file is open.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
